stages.implementation_notes:
dune-project: add a (package (name pera-core) ...) stanza with depends ocaml (>= 5.4), pera-types (= :version), pera-provider (= :version), containers (>= 3.0), yojson (>= 2.0), eio/eio_main/eio_linux (>= 1.0), and with-test qcheck-core/qcheck-alcotest + alcotest. generate_opam_files is already on, so pera-core.opam is produced by dune — do not hand-write it. lib/pera_core/dune: (library (name pera_core) (public_name pera-core) (libraries pera_types pera_provider containers yojson eio eio_main eio_linux)).

Define in agent_types.mli (open Pera_types and Pera_provider where needed in the .ml):
(1) agent_message — the §3 superset. Recommended shape that realises 'permits synthetic messages, none in v1, but the shape exists':
    type synthetic = |   (* no synthetic message kinds in v1; compaction summaries / model-change markers added at M6 *)
    type agent_message = Real of Provider.message | Synthetic of synthetic
  The empty `synthetic` makes the Synthetic case uninhabited now, so convert_to_llm can match `Real m -> [m] | Synthetic _ -> .` (refutation). THIS ENCODING IS SETTLED (user decision) — do NOT substitute a plain `type agent_message = Provider.message` alias or an independent mirror type. Rationale: it honours spec §3 ('superset… permits synthetic messages', 'not the same type') and the closed-sum discipline of §3 Events, and makes the M6 compaction change additive (add a `synthetic` constructor + render it as a framed user message per §8) rather than an alias→variant refactor. The wrap/unwrap boilerplate is the accepted cost. This wrapping of Provider.message is also what places these types in pera_core (it references pera_provider), consistent with §2 layering.
(2) tool_output: type tool_output = Tool_text of string | Tool_json of Yojson.Safe.t. (The loop converts this to Types.tool_result_content: Tool_text s -> content = `String s; Tool_json j -> content = j.)
(3) 'ctx tool (spec §3): { name: string; description: string; schema: Json_schema.t; mode: [ `Sequential | `Parallel ]; execute: ctx:'ctx -> args:Yojson.Safe.t -> sw:Eio.Switch.t -> cancel:Eio.Cancel.t -> (tool_output, Types.tool_error) result }. mode is the per-tool default execution mode (spec §9): read/grep Parallel, write/bash Sequential.
(4) agent_event (port of vendor/pi/packages/agent/src/types.ts AgentEvent lines 403-418):
    type agent_event =
      | AE_agent_start
      | AE_agent_end of { messages : agent_message list }
      | AE_turn_start
      | AE_turn_end of { message : agent_message; tool_results : Types.tool_result_content list }
      | AE_message_start of { message : agent_message }
      | AE_message_update of { message : agent_message; event : Types.assistant_message_event }
      | AE_message_end of { message : agent_message }
      | AE_tool_execution_start of { tool_call_id : string; tool_name : string; args : Yojson.Safe.t }
      | AE_tool_execution_update of { tool_call_id : string; tool_name : string; partial : Yojson.Safe.t }
      | AE_tool_execution_end of { tool_call_id : string; tool_name : string; result : Yojson.Safe.t; is_error : bool }
(5) before_tool_call_result: type before_tool_call_result = Allow | Deny of string.
(6) turn_update (port of AgentLoopTurnUpdate): { messages : agent_message list option; model : Types.model option; thinking : bool option } — any field None means leave unchanged.
Provide helper(s): convert tool_output -> Types.tool_result_content given a tool_call_id and is_error. Keep these types in .mli; .ml just realises them. open Containers in the .ml. No structural equality; if tests need equality on events, expose an `equal`/`pp` for agent_event (Alcotest testable) — see test-patterns §2.

Port vendor/pi/packages/agent/src/agent-loop.ts (outer loop 169-266, inner loop 174-254) and config from types.ts (AgentLoopConfig 135-277). Define in agent_loop.mli:

type 'ctx agent_loop_config = {
  model : Types.model;
  system : string;
  options : Provider.simple_stream_options;
  stream_fn : <the seam type from Stage 4>;
  convert_to_llm : agent_message list -> Provider.message list;
  tool_ctx : 'ctx;
  tools : 'ctx Agent_types.tool list;
  tool_execution : [ `Sequential | `Parallel ];   (* default `Parallel *)
  transform_context : (agent_message list -> agent_message list) option;
  get_api_key : (provider:string -> string option) option;
  before_tool_call : (* see Stage 6 *) ... option;
  after_tool_call : (* see Stage 6 *) ... option;
  should_stop_after_turn : (should_stop_ctx -> bool) option;
  prepare_next_turn : (prepare_ctx -> Agent_types.turn_update option) option;
  get_steering_messages : (unit -> agent_message list) option;
  get_follow_up_messages : (unit -> agent_message list) option;
}
(Declare the before_/after_tool_call hook field TYPES now even though execution is wired in Stage 6, so the config type is stable across stages; their context record types can be defined here. should_stop_ctx / prepare_ctx carry at least: the latest assistant message, the tool_results of the turn, and the current message list — mirror pi's ShouldStopAfterTurnContext / PrepareNextTurnContext.)

val run : 'ctx agent_loop_config -> messages:agent_message list -> sw:Eio.Switch.t -> (agent_event, agent_message list) Event_stream.t
run forks a fibre under ~sw that drives the loop and pushes agent_events into the returned Event_stream; the stream resolves (close) with the final agent_message list on agent_end. (Same fork-and-return shape as Anthropic_provider.stream_simple / Faux_provider.) The caller consumes events and reads the final messages via Event_stream.result.

Control flow (spec §5 'Control flow — outer and inner loops'):
- Emit AE_agent_start once.
- OUTER loop: run the INNER loop; when it exits, call get_follow_up_messages; if it returns messages, they become the next pending messages and the inner loop restarts; else emit AE_agent_end {messages} and close the stream.
- INNER loop iterates while there are tool calls to chase OR pending steering/initial messages:
  1. If there are pending messages (initial prompt, steering, or follow-up), append them to context.messages as Real (Provider.UserMessage ...).
  2. Emit AE_turn_start. Build the provider context: apply transform_context (if set) to the agent_message list, run convert_to_llm to get Provider.message list, set context = { system; messages; tools = schemas-from-config-tools; thinking = options-driven }. Resolve get_api_key (if set) — for M2 with Faux it is unused but must be CALLED (the contract). Call stream_fn ~model ~context ~options ~sw.
  3. Consume the provider Event_stream: append a placeholder partial assistant message as the last entry of context.messages; for each AME_* event, replace that last entry with AME's partial snapshot and emit AE_message_update {message; event}. Emit AE_message_start at the first event and AE_message_end at the terminal event. On AME_done, swap in the final message; on AME_error, treat as a terminal assistant message with stop_reason Error/Aborted.
  4. If stop_reason is Error or Aborted: emit AE_turn_end {message; tool_results=[]} and terminate the whole run (emit AE_agent_end, close).
  5. (Stage 6 inserts tool execution here.) For THIS stage: if the assistant message has no tool calls (text-only / EndTurn), tool_results = []. Emit AE_turn_end {message; tool_results}.
  6. Call should_stop_after_turn (if set); if true, exit inner loop (-> outer loop will check follow-ups; note pi semantics: should_stop true terminates the run unless follow-ups exist).
  7. Call prepare_next_turn (if set); apply any turn_update (swap messages/model/thinking).
  8. Call get_steering_messages (if set); captured messages become pending for the next inner iteration.
  9. Inner loop continues only if there are pending steering messages or pending tool calls; text-only EndTurn with no steering exits the inner loop.

KEEP THE LOOP PURE-FROM-IO: it never touches piaf, files, or a clock; everything external is a config callback. Hooks may not raise (raising propagates out of run — programmer error, per spec §5). Use let*/Result combinators and library functions over match on option/result (pera-specific §3). Mutation is confined to the one context value for the run's duration (spec 'State ownership').

This stage's tests use text-only Faux scripts.
